<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">14278232</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2138007</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>PATHOGENESIS OF INFLAMMATION </article-title><subtitle>II. IN VIVO OBSERVATIONS OF THE INFLAMMATORY EFFECTS OF ACTIVATED HAGEMAN FACTOR AND BRADYKININ</subtitle></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Graham</surname><given-names>Richard C.</given-names><suffix>Jr.</suffix></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ebert</surname><given-names>Robert H.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ratnoff</surname><given-names>Oscar D.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Moses</surname><given-names>John M.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x34f7ee0N0x32c2678">From the Department of Medicine, Western Reserve University, Cleveland</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>5</month><year>1965</year></pub-date><volume>121</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>807</fpage><lpage>818</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>18</day><month>11</month><year>1964</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1965 by The Rockefeller Institute</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Activated Hageman factor, when injected into the rabbit ear chamber, produces a delayed and prolonged inflammatory response characterized by prominent sticking and emigration of leucocytes. In contrast, preformed bradykinin evokes an immediate and more transient response in which leucocytic emigration occurs less frequently. It is concluded that either Hageman factor produces its inflammatory effects by mechanisms other than kinin release, or bradykinin released endogenously has effects quite different from those resulting from a single injection of the exogenous material. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>